{"conjecture": "Long-running software systems degrade through the silent accumulation of 'constraint residue'—the local assumptions, backwards-compatibility shims, and circumstantial workarounds left behind by each correct change. These residues act as an ever-growing web of implicit, undocumented dependencies that make new modifications locally testable but globally incompressible, shrinking the space of viable future changes without violating any single metric of code health. The system becomes harder to modify not because any individual change introduced a defect, but because each change's context-specific constraints crystallize into permanent architecture, gradually transforming a flexible system into a rigid fossil of its own history."}
